NPU telemetry without xrt-smi: XRT's in-process query API and Windows' GPU-engine statistics,
probed while rewriting tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.cpp into a live NPU monitor.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07 18:40-19:07, XRT 2.21.0 (SDK at
C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt), NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, firmware 1.5.5.391, Windows 11 Pro
26200.9168, power mode Default throughout. A wide_resnet101_2 AdaRound quantize (8 pinned CPU
threads) ran concurrently the whole time; nothing below is a latency number, so it is noted,
not a confound.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
The question asked was "are there other ways to get data points from the NPU that don't
involve xrt-smi". Everything this repo had read live from the NPU so far came from xrt-smi
(`tools/session_hold.py`, `results/gops_*.log`, `xrt_smi_platform_pmode.log`), and
tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.py/.cpp's own docstring recorded that HWiNFO's native NPU readings
are placeholders (0.001 V, a flat 800 MHz) and that its D3D/Compute0 usage read 0 % under a
held VitisAI EP session. Two candidate sources were tested here: XRT's own query API
(pyxrt / xrt::device::get_info -- the interface xrt-smi is a CLI over) and the Windows
GPU-engine statistics (PDH / D3DKMT, what Task Manager reads). Both answered.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
NPU load for the "busy" samples: mlir-aie v1.4.2 whole_array.py (ironenv), bf16->f32, 2048^3,
m=64 k=64 n=64 with the local --c-single-buffer 1 patch, --warmup 3 --iters 5000 (about 45 s
of back-to-back dispatches; 2293.79-2342.27 GFLOPS across the runs), started from Git Bash:
  python whole_array.py --dev npu -M 2048 -K 2048 -N 2048 -m 64 -k 64 -n 64 --dtype_in bf16 \
      --dtype_out f32 --c-single-buffer 1 --warmup 3 --iters 5000
Probes:
  (a) pyxrt (C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\python\pyxrt.pyd): pyxrt.device(0).get_info(<every
      member of pyxrt.xrt_info_device>), timed, during the load.
  (b) PowerShell Get-Counter '\GPU Engine(*)\Utilization Percentage', '\GPU Adapter Memory(*)\
      Shared Usage' / 'Dedicated Usage', during the load.
  (c) a 40-line C++ probe (xrt::device::get_info<...> for name, bdf, max_clock_frequency_mhz,
      platform, host, dynamic_regions, aie, aie_shim, aie_mem, electrical, thermal, memory),
      MSVC 2022 Build Tools, boost 1.92 headers from a new conda env `npu_monitor_build`
      (conda-forge libboost-headers + nlohmann_json; the SDK's xrt/detail/any.h needs
      boost/any.hpp), linked against xrt_coreutil.lib (the DLL is C:\Windows\System32\
      xrt_coreutil.dll, installed by the NPU driver). Run idle, then every 3 s during the load,
      then idle again.
  (d) the rewritten tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe itself (--once, --plain, --json), idle and
      during the load, with its HWiNFO registry output read back.
  (e) three attempts to put a VitisAI EP session on the NPU for the same PDH check -- all
      failed for reasons unrelated to the probe (below), so the VitisAI path is NOT covered.

