(e) VitisAI EP: not covered
===========================
Three attempts to hold a VitisAI EP (ORT) session on the NPU so the same engine counter could
be read under it, all in resnet_env17 with RYZEN_AI_INSTALLATION_PATH=C:\Program Files\RyzenAI\1.7.1:
  1. tools/session_hold.py --model models/yolov8n_cut_xint8.onnx --cache-key yolocutcachekey --streams 1
       -> "check failure: node != nullptrcannot find producer. onnx_node_arg_name=/model.21/m.0/cv2/act/Mul_output_0_DequantizeLinear_Output/duplicated"
  2. tools/session_hold.py --model models/resnet50_xint8_c64.onnx --cache-key modelcachekey --streams 1 --hold 35
       -> every sample "npu memory: 0 MB  gops: 0  measured completions: 0.0/s"; xrt-smi showed no
          hardware context; the session never ran on the NPU (not diagnosed).
  3. pipelines/resnet50/4_run.py --ep npu --images data/calib --n 6000 --model models/resnet50_xint8_c64.onnx
       -> onnxruntime RUNTIME_EXCEPTION in vitis_ai_ep_1: "vaip_core::TarWriter::write(...)"
So the earlier observation in hwinfo_npu_bridge.py's docstring -- HWiNFO's D3D/Compute0 at 0 %
under a held VitisAI session -- is neither confirmed nor refuted here. What IS established is
that an XRT hardware context created through IRON/pyxrt is fully visible to the engine counter.
Whether the VitisAI EP's contexts (also XRT hardware contexts underneath) show the same is the
open item; the compile caches were being used by another session at the time and were not
touched with --fresh to find out.

Also found, not used: the XRT SDK ships nputrace_tool/ (an ETW session over the NPU driver's
WPP provider abcf72e0-3618-40c8-ad95-eddd971fd3c9, via tracelog/tracefmt; needs Administrator)
and xdp_aie_{profile,trace,halt}_plugin.dll (XRT's XDP AIE profile/trace, driven by xrt.ini
[AIE_profile_settings] / [AIE_trace_settings]) -- both are trace tooling, not a monitor feed.

THE FINDING
===========
1. The Windows GPU-engine statistics see the NPU. It is an MCDM adapter with its own LUID
   (0x0000d6bf here), one engine (eng_0, engtype compute), a shared-memory segment, and per-
   process utilization -- 88.5 % / 84-87 % across the IRON GEMM run, 0 % idle, memory equal
   to xrt-smi's figure. This is the only live utilization number for the NPU on this stack;
   xrt-smi's GOPS/FPS/latency read N/A for the same context. DXCore lists the adapter under
   DXCORE_HARDWARE_TYPE_ATTRIBUTE_NPU as "NPU Compute Accelerator Device" (not under
   D3D12_CORE_COMPUTE, which returns only the iGPU and the basic render driver).
2. XRT's max_clock_frequency_mhz is a live clock readback on this driver: 800 MHz idle,
   1800 MHz for as long as a hardware context is active, 800 again after, at ~0.05 ms per
   query. The earlier note that it "reads 800 in every mode" was an idle reading. It agrees
   with the in-kernel cycle-counter clock probe (1.80 GHz busy in default mode) the other
   session ran the same day.
3. The rest of XRT's query surface on this driver: name, bdf, platform (columns, power mode),
   host (XRT/driver versions), pcie_info answer; aie / aie_shim / aie_mem / memory return
   "No such query request"; electrical fails at the driver escape (0xc0000023); thermal and
   mechanical report "No sensors present". The per-context table (aie-partitions) is xrt-smi
   only. Voltage and power remain unavailable from every documented interface.
4. The monitor built on all three (tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.cpp): per poll it costs one
   xrt-smi process (~120-170 ms), one PDH collection (~0.7 ms) and two XRT queries (~0.2 ms).

NOT TESTED
==========
- The engine counter under a VitisAI EP session (e above).
- Whether max_clock_frequency_mhz reports the balanced/powersaver clocks (1.03 / 0.80 GHz
  per the other session's probe) under load in those modes; only `Default` was sampled.
- The dashboard (VT redraw) mode was exercised only through its plain and --once renderers
  in this log; the escape-sequence path ran in the user's console window, not captured here.
